---
import evaluation from '../ml/evaluation.json';
import model from '../ml/model.json';

const { training } = model;
const sessionsPerCase = evaluation[0].sessions;

function outcome(alerts: number, sessions: number, medianDelay: number | null, alertExpected: boolean) {
  if (!alertExpected) {
    return alerts === 0 ? `No false alerts in ${sessions}` : `${alerts} false alerts in ${sessions}`;
  }
  const delay = medianDelay === null ? '' : `, median ${medianDelay} s into recovery`;
  return `Caught ${alerts} of ${sessions}${delay}`;
}
---
<section class="section" id="analysis">
  <p class="eyebrow">On-board AI</p>
  <h2 class="section-title">Machine learning that runs where the astronaut is.</h2>
  <p>
    The multispectral sensor on the astronaut's wearable streams its readings to a server
    on board the spacecraft. There, a machine-learning model learns that astronaut's own
    patterns and reads every new window against them. Nothing has to reach Earth for an
    answer to come back.
  </p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Can this reading be trusted?</h3>
      <p>
        Lost contact, missing data and motion are detected first. An unreliable window is
        never scored; the astronaut is asked to adjust the sensor and measure again.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>How does it compare with this person?</h3>
      <p>
        A neural network predicts this astronaut's heart rate and skin temperature for the
        current moment of the activity. It is told the schedule, never the expected values:
        it learns those, and how much they normally vary, from reference sessions.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Does the change persist?</h3>
      <p>
        A single odd reading is not an alert. Spectra asks for verification only when a
        deviation holds across consecutive windows, and it says which signal changed and
        for how long.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="status">
    <div class="status-card">
      <p class="status-label">Running in this demo</p>
      <h3>A small neural network</h3>
      <p>
        Two hidden layers and {training.parameters} parameters, trained beforehand
        on {training.sessions} synthetic sessions of normal recovery and run in your browser
        for inference only. Its inputs are heart rate and skin temperature.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="status-card">
      <p class="status-label">Designed for the product</p>
      <h3>The same model, on the spacecraft</h3>
      <p>
        Trained on each astronaut's measured reference sessions and run on the on-board
        server. Motion and the multispectral channels become inputs once measured data
        shows they help; we have not tested that yet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">Does the model add anything?</h3>
  <p>
    We ran the model and a plain statistical baseline on {sessionsPerCase} sessions per
    case. None of these sessions was used to train or tune the model. Both use the same
    rule: three consecutive windows above the threshold.
  </p>
  <table class="eval-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Case</th>
        <th scope="col">Statistical baseline</th>
        <th scope="col">On-board model</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {evaluation.map((row) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{row.label}</th>
          <td data-column="Statistical baseline">
            {outcome(row.baseline.alerts, row.sessions, row.baseline.medianDelay, row.alertExpected)}
          </td>
          <td data-column="On-board model">
            {outcome(row.model.alerts, row.sessions, row.model.medianDelay, row.alertExpected)}
          </td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="source">
    Read this as a technical result. Every session is synthetic, and the simulator ties
    skin temperature to effort, which the model benefits from. Zero false alerts
    in {sessionsPerCase} sessions does not mean the rate is zero. None of this is clinical
    performance; it shows the pipeline works and what to measure next.
  </p>
</section>
